"On $e$-positivity of trees and connected partitions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12934)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Authors: Foster Tom\nDate: Thu Sep 19 17:45:21 2024 ... This brings us very close to the conjecture by Dahlberg, She, and van Willigenburg of non-$e$-positivity for all trees with a vertex of degree at least four. We also prove that spiders with four legs cannot have an $e$-positive chromatic symmetric function.\nTitle: On $e$-positivity of trees and connected partitions\nAuthors: Foster Tom\nDate: Thu Sep 19 17:45:21 2024\n\nWe prove that a tree with a vertex of degree at least five must be missing a connected partition of some type and therefore its chromatic symmetric function cannot be $e$-positive. We prove that this also holds for a tree with a vertex of degree four as long as it is not adjacent to any leaf. This brings us very close to the conjecture by Dahlberg, She, and van Willigenburg of non-$e$-positivity for all trees with a vertex of degree at least four. We also prove that spiders with four legs cannot have an $e$-positive chromatic symmetric function.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSchur positivity from signed elementary expansions: clique-spiders and spiders $S(a,b,2)$ (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22184)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Date: Sun Aug 23 02:44:53 2026 ... Finally, we prove that the spider $S(a,b,2)$ for $a\\ge b\\ge2$ with $3\\nmid b$ is $e$-positive if and only if $(a,b)\\in\\{(6,4),(12,4),(9,7)\\}$, which advances the study of Tom's conjecture concerning the $e$-positivity of spiders $S(a,b,2)$.\nTitle: Schur positivity from signed elementary expansions: clique-spiders and spiders $S(a,b,2)$\nAuthors: David G. L. Wang, Watson Z. Y. Wang\nDate: Sun Aug 23 02:44:53 2026\n\nWe prove a Schur alpha-omega lemma for chromatic symmetric functions. It bounds the partitions indexing nonzero Schur coefficients in terms of higher independence numbers, higher clique numbers, and the chromatic number. We then establish three equivalent dominance-matching criteria: matrix, Hall, and order-ideal, that certify Schur positivity from a fixed signed $e_I$-expansion. As applications, we obtain complete classifications of $e$-positivity and Schur positivity for four basic families of $3$-clique-spiders. Here $S^{ghk}_{rst}$ is formed by joining a common center to one vertex of each of $K_r$, $K_s$, and $K_t$ by internally disjoint paths of lengths $g$, $h$, and $k$, respectively. As a result, $S^{000}_{rst}$ is Schur positive exactly when $r\\ge st-1$, and every graph $S^{100}_{rst}$ and $S^{010}_{rst}$ is Schur positive. When $s=t$, the graph $S^{001}_{rst}$ is Schur positive; when $s>t$, its Schur-positive members fall into four explicit parameter regimes. We also introduce a path-clique bootstrap and use it to prove that every spider $S(a,b,2)$ is Schur positive. Finally, we prove that the spider $S(a,b,2)$ for $a\\ge b\\ge2$ with $3\\nmid b$ is $e$-positive if and only if $(a,b)\\in\\{(6,4),(12,4),(9,7)\\}$, which advances the study of Tom's conjecture concerning the $e$-positivity of spiders $S(a,b,2)$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ne-positivity conjecture for the sporadic spiders S(b²+b,b,1) | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/352905/e-positivity-conjecture-for-the-sporadic-spiders-s-b2-b-b-1)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Spider e-positivity conjecture. ... Wang, “The e-positivity and Schur positivity of the chromatic symmetric functions of some trees”, arXiv:2112.06619 (2021).\n\n# \n\ne-positivity conjecture for the sporadic spiders S(b²+b,b,1)\n\nFor integers $a,b$a,b with $a\\ge b\\ge 2$a≥b≥2, let $S(a,b,1)$S(a,b,1) be the spider formed from paths of lengths corresponding to the three parameters, with a common endpoint. A graph is $e$e-positive when its chromatic symmetric function has a nonnegative expansion in the elementary symmetric-function basis.\n\nSpider e-positivity conjecture. If $b$b is even and $b\\not\\equiv2\\pmod3$b≡2(mod 3), then the spider $S(b^2+b,\\,b,\\,1)$S(b 2+b,b,1) is $e$e-positive.\n\nThe conjecture is proposed because $a=b^2+b$a=b 2+b is a sporadic case in the paper’s bounds for $S(a,b,1)$S(a,b,1). The source reports that it has been checked for $b\\in\\{4,6\\}$b∈{4,6}, while no general proof is stated.\n\n\n## Primary source\n\nDavid G. L. Wang and Monica M. Y. Wang, “The e-positivity and Schur positivity of the chromatic symmetric functions of some trees”, arXiv:2112.06619 (2021).\n\nchromatic-symmetric-functionse-positivitytrees\n\n0\n\n2 weeks ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the diameter of Schrijver graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.01884)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.9 years ago; Title: On the diameter of Schrijver graphs\nAuthors: Agustina Victoria Ledezma, Adrián Pastine, Pablo Torres, Mario Valencia-Pabon ... In this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ with $r \\geq 1$.\nTitle: On the diameter of Schrijver graphs\nAuthors: Agustina Victoria Ledezma, Adrián Pastine, Pablo Torres, Mario Valencia-Pabon\nDate: Fri Dec  3 12:48:33 2021\n\nFor $k \\geq 1$ and $n \\geq 2k$, the well known Kneser graph $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ has all $k$-element subsets of an $n$-element set as vertices; two such subsets are adjacent if they are disjoint. Schrijver constructed a vertex-critical subgraph $\\operatorname{SG}(n,k)$ of $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ with the same chromatic number. In this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ with $r \\geq 1$. We obtain an exact value of the diameter of $\\operatorname{