meter of Schrijver graphs ... In this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ with $r \\geq 1$. ... Adri´an Pastine †Pablo Torres ‡Mario Valencia-Pabon§\n\nPreprint PDF Available\n\n# On the diameter of Schrijver graphs\n\n  * December 2021\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2112.01884\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nAdrian Pastine\n\n  * National University of San Luis\n\nPablo Torres\n\n  * National University of Rosario\n\nMario Valencia-Pabon\n\n  * University of Lorraine\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor $k \\geq 1$ and $n \\geq 2k$, the well known Kneser graph $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ has all k-element subsets of an n-element set as vertices; two such subsets are adjacent if they are disjoint. Schrijver constructed a vertex-critical subgraph $\\operatorname{SG}(n,k)$ of $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ with the same chromatic number. In this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ with $r \\geq 1$. We obtain that the diameter of $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ is equal to 2 if $r \\geq 2k-2$; 3 if $k-2 \\leq r \\leq 2k-3$; k if r=1; and for $2 \\leq r \\leq k-3$, we obtain that the diameter of $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ is at most equal to $k-r+1$.\n\n\narXiv:2112.01884v1 [math.CO] 3 Dec 2021\n\nOn the diameter of Schrijver graphs∗\n\nAdri´an Pastine †Pablo Torres ‡Mario Valencia-Pabon§\n\nAbstract\n\nFor k≥1 and n≥2 k, the well known Kneser graph KG(n, k) has all k-element subsets\n\nof an n-element set as vertices; two such subsets are adjacent if they are disjoint. Schrijver\n\nconstructed a vertex-critical subgraph SG(n, k) of KG(n, k) with the same chromatic number.\n\nIn this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph SG(2 k+r, k) with r≥1. We obtain that\n\nthe diameter of SG(2 k+r, k) is equal to 2 if r≥2 k−2; 3 if k−2≤r≤2 k−3; k if r= 1; and\n\nfor 2 ≤r≤k−3, we obtain that the diameter of SG(2 k+r, k) is at most equal to k−r+ 1.\n\nKeywords: Schrijver graphs, Diameter of graphs.\n\n1 Introduction\n\nLet G be a connected graph. Given two vertices a, b ∈G, dist(a, b), the distance between a and b,\n\nis deﬁned as the length of the shortest path in G joining a to b. The diameter of G, that we denote\n\nby D(G), is deﬁned as the maximum distance between any pair of vertices in G.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe spiders $S(4m+2,\\,2m,\\,1)$ are $e$-positive (https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.04915)\nciteturn1academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Title: The spiders $S(4m+2,\\,2m,\\,1)$ are $e$-positive ... Date: Wed May  8 09:35:40 2024\n\nBy using the composition method, we establish the $e$-positivity of spider graphs of the form $S(4m+2,\\, 2m,\\, 1)$, which was conjectured by Aliniaeifard, Wang and van Willigenburg.\nTitle: The spiders $S(4m+2,\\,2m,\\,1)$ are $e$-positive\nAuthors: Davion Q. B. Tang, David G. L. Wang, Monica M. Y. Wang\nDate: Wed May  8 09:35:40 2024\n\nBy using the composition method, we establish the $e$-positivity of spider graphs of the form $S(4m+2,\\, 2m,\\, 1)$, which was conjectured by Aliniaeifard, Wang and van Willigenburg. We expand chromatic symmetric functions in terms of $e_I$ for compositions $I$. Following the divide-and-conquer strategy, we group one or two $e_J$-terms that have positive coefficients with each $e_I$-term that has a negative coefficient, where the compositions $J$ are selected to be distinct compositions obtained by rearranging the parts of $I$, and show the positivity of the sum of those coefficients.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLaboratoire d'informatique de Paris Nord (https://lipn.univ-paris13.fr/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/RAPPORT_17_22_CALIN_big.pdf)\nciteturn1search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Bounds on the diameter of graph associa- ... Pastine, P. ... On the diamet