n identify when the chromatic symmetric function of a spider, which is a tree consisting of multiple paths all adjacent at a leaf to a center vertex, is not e-positive. These conditions generalize to trees and graphs with cut vertices as well. Finally, by using a result of Orellana and Scott, we give a method to inductively calculate certain coefficients in the elementary symmetric function expansion of the chromatic symmetric function of a spider, leading to further e-positivity conditions for spiders.\n\n## Keywords\n\nGraph theory\n\n;\n\nSymmetric functions\n\n;\n\nChromatic symmetric functions\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncombinatorial theory 5 (2) (2025), #11 (https://escholarship.org/content/qt9dc0v7wb/qt9dc0v7wb_noSplash_28ede5a36d36f74b24d311c99b18bfbc.pdf)\nciteturn1search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Wang and M. ... The e-positivity and Schur positivity of the chromatic\nsymmetric functions of some trees. ... On the e-positivity of trees and spiders.\nmutations with restricted position. J. Combin. Theory Ser. A, 62(2), 1993. doi:\n10.1016/0097-3165(93)90048-d.\n[ST24]\nB. Sagan and F. Tom. Chromatic symmetric functions and change of basis. 2024.\n[Sta89]\nR. Stanley. Log-concave and unimodal sequences in algebra, combinatorics, and\ngeometry. Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences, 576, 1989. doi:10.1111/\nj.1749-6632.1989.tb16434.x.\n[Sta95]\nR. Stanley. A symmetric function generalization of the chromatic polynomial. Adv.\nMath., 111(1), 1995. doi:10.1006/aima.1995.1020.\n[Sta98]\nR. Stanley. Graph colorings and related symmetric functions: ideas and applica-\ntions. Discrete Math., 193(1), 1998. doi:10.1016/s0012-365x(98)00146-0.\n[SV21]\nB. Sagan and V. Vatter. Bijective proofs of proper coloring theorems. The American\nMathematical Monthly, 128(6), 2021. doi:10.1080/00029890.2021.1901460.\n[SW12]\nJ. Shareshian and M. Wachs. Chromatic quasisymmetric functions and Hessenberg\nv